---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import WaterCycleSim from '../../simulators/watercycle/WaterCycleSim.astro';
import { waterCycleQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/watercycle';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is the water cycle?' },
  { id: 'stores', label: 'Where Earth’s water is' },
  { id: 'processes', label: 'The processes' },
  { id: 'land', label: 'How the land changes the cycle' },
  { id: 'times', label: 'How long water stays' },
  { id: 'climate', label: 'The water cycle and climate' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'U.S. Geological Survey. The Water Cycle.', url: 'https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/water-cycle' },
  { text: 'Shiklomanov, I. A. (1993). World fresh water resources. In P. H. Gleick (Ed.), Water in Crisis. Oxford University Press.' },
  { text: 'Trenberth, K. E., Smith, L., Qian, T., Dai, A., & Fasullo, J. (2007). Estimates of the global water budget and its annual cycle using observational and model data. Journal of Hydrometeorology, 8(4), 758–769.' },
  { text: 'NASA Earth Observatory. The Water Cycle.', url: 'https://earthobservatory.nasa.gov/features/Water' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="earth-science"
  topic="water-cycle"
  description="Interactive water cycle simulator: follow water through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff, infiltration and transpiration, and see how sunshine, wind and ground cover change it."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={waterCycleQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      The water in your glass may once have been part of a cloud, a glacier, a river or even a dinosaur. Water is
      constantly on the move between the ocean, the air, the land and underground. Change the sunshine, wind and ground
      cover and follow hundreds of drops around the cycle.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <WaterCycleSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Watch drops <strong>evaporate</strong> from the ocean, rise and <strong>condense</strong> into clouds.</li>
      <li>See clouds drift inland and <strong>rain or snow</strong> most heavily as they rise over the mountain.</li>
      <li>Follow water on land: some <strong>runs off</strong> into the river, some <strong>soaks in</strong> to become groundwater, and some is taken up by plants and <strong>transpired</strong>.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>sunshine</strong>: weak sun builds up snow on the mountain; strong sun speeds evaporation and melts it.</li>
      <li>Switch the <strong>ground cover</strong> to City and compare the runoff and infiltration numbers.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is the water cycle?</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>water cycle</strong> (or hydrological cycle) is the continuous movement of water around Earth. Water
    changes state between liquid, gas (water vapour) and solid (ice and snow), and moves between the oceans, atmosphere,
    land, living things and rocks underground. It is powered by the <strong>Sun’s energy</strong>, which evaporates water,
    and by <strong>gravity</strong>, which pulls rain, rivers and groundwater back down. The total amount of water stays
    essentially the same; it just keeps moving.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stores">Where Earth’s water is</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Share of all water</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Oceans</td><td>about 96.5 %</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ice caps and glaciers</td><td>about 1.7 %</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Groundwater</td><td>about 1.7 %</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lakes, rivers, soil</td><td>under 0.1 %</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Atmosphere</td><td>about 0.001 %</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Less than 1 % of all water is liquid fresh water that people can easily use.</p>

  <h2 id="processes">The processes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Evaporation:</strong> the Sun heats water, which turns into invisible water vapour. Most comes from the oceans.</li>
    <li><strong>Transpiration:</strong> plants release water vapour from their leaves. Together with evaporation this is called evapotranspiration.</li>
    <li><strong>Condensation:</strong> rising vapour cools to its dew point and forms cloud droplets.</li>
    <li><strong>Precipitation:</strong> droplets grow and fall as rain, snow, sleet or hail.</li>
    <li><strong>Infiltration:</strong> water soaks into the soil and down into the rocks, becoming <strong>groundwater</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Runoff:</strong> water flows over the surface into streams and rivers, and back to the sea.</li>
    <li><strong>Melting and sublimation:</strong> snow and ice melt into water, or turn directly into vapour.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="land">How the land changes the cycle</h2>
  <p>
    What happens to rain depends on where it lands. Forests intercept rain with their leaves, their roots open up the
    soil so water soaks in, and they return large amounts of vapour to the air by transpiration. Bare or compacted soil
    lets more water run off. In cities, roofs and roads are <strong>impermeable</strong>, so rain rushes into drains and
    rivers within minutes, raising the risk of flash floods and reducing the groundwater that keeps rivers flowing in dry
    weather. Mountains force moist air to rise, so the side facing the wind is much wetter than the sheltered “rain
    shadow” beyond.
  </p>

  <h2 id="times">How long water stays</h2>
  <p>
    Water passes quickly through some parts of the cycle and very slowly through others. A water molecule spends on
    average about 9 days in the atmosphere, weeks to months in rivers, decades to centuries in lakes, around 3,000 years in
    the oceans, and up to tens of thousands of years in deep groundwater or ice sheets.
  </p>

  <h2 id="climate">The water cycle and climate</h2>
  <p>
    Evaporation takes in heat and condensation releases it, so the water cycle moves huge amounts of energy around the
    planet, warming high latitudes and powering storms. Warmer air can hold about 7 % more water vapour for each degree
    Celsius, so as the climate warms the water cycle intensifies: heavier downpours in some places and more severe
    droughts in others.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Water vapour</dt>
    <dd>Water as an invisible gas.</dd>
    <dt>Groundwater</dt>
    <dd>Water stored in the spaces in soil and rocks below the water table.</dd>
    <dt>Catchment</dt>
    <dd>The area of land that drains into a particular river.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Clouds are made of steam or vapour.”</strong> Clouds are liquid droplets or ice crystals; vapour is invisible.</li>
    <li><strong>“Groundwater flows in underground rivers.”</strong> Mostly it seeps slowly through tiny gaps in soil and rock.</li>
    <li><strong>“Water used up by people is gone forever.”</strong> It returns to the cycle, though it may become polluted or move somewhere less useful.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The Sun’s energy and gravity drive water around the cycle.</li>
    <li>Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, runoff and transpiration move water between stores.</li>
    <li>Ground cover strongly affects how much rain soaks in versus runs off.</li>
    <li>Water is conserved: the same water is used again and again.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: each drop stands for a large amount of water, and the rates are chosen to show the processes clearly rather than to match a real place.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
